<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    The live Claude lane's own code (CARD-1545; W2 slice S21 of docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-21-w2-nightly-port-slices.md):
    the run admission and its outcome classifier, the native runner's child environment, and the protected-input pin.
    The owner's decision (2026-11-26): the source files behind the lane's digest live in one isolated project dedicated to
    running real Claude. tests/live-lane-inputs.json names this directory and the build inputs below as the lane's
    contractFiles, so the lane fingerprint moves only when live-lane code moves.

    Self-contained on purpose: no ProjectReference and no PackageReference (the framework only), and no Compile item
    outside this directory. The CLI (Antiphon.Scripts) references this project and binds its seams; the direction is
    Scripts -> LiveLane, never the reverse. LiveLaneProjectIsolationTests holds both rules.

    Build inputs that can change this project's output, all named in contractFiles: this directory (its sources and this
    file), the repository's Directory.Build.props, Directory.Build.targets (and the tests/Shared/CheckpointRepeat.targets
    it imports), Directory.Build.rsp, global.json (the SDK) and .editorconfig (analyzer severities). There is no NuGet.config
    or Directory.Packages.props in the repository, and no package to restore.

    S22 (receipt, attestation, fingerprint and status, with the Claude version as its own axis) adds its code here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Antiphon.LiveLane</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Antiphon.LiveLane</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
